Agreed — fixed in 758f12cf with `api_tab_close_last_tab_of_background_workspace_keeps_focus`.

Worth noting how the test is shaped, because the obvious two-workspace version does **not** actually pin the behaviour you named. With workspaces `[background, active]` and `active` selected, closing `background`'s last tab leaves the survivor at index 0 — which is exactly where `self.state.selected = ws_idx` already points. Focus lands correctly by coincidence, not by reconciliation.

I verified that: deleting the focus-restore block in `close_selected_workspace`

```rust
// Keep focus on the previously focused workspace
if let Some(id) = active_workspace_id {
    if let Some(idx) = self.workspaces.iter().position(|ws| ws.id == id) {
        self.selected = idx;
    }
}
```

left all seven `api_tab_close` tests green with the two-workspace version.

So the committed test uses three workspaces — `[background, middle, active]` with `active` at index 2 — and closes `background`'s only tab. The survivor `active` shifts from index 2 to index 1, so `selected = ws_idx` (0) is now wrong and the reconciliation has to do real work.

Mutation results against the committed test:

| Mutation | Result |
| --- | --- |
| drop the focus-restore block in `close_selected_workspace` | **only** this test fails |
| drop `self.state.selected = ws_idx` in the cascade branch | this test fails (also caught by the linked-child test) |

Full suite: 3090 passed, 0 skipped.
